    },
    "body": "Ignore this it's been fixed. \n\n----------\n\n\nThis PR introduces a breaking change to `popovertarget` button behaviour and I want to bring this up for wider discussion.\r\n\r\nThere's essentially 8 states to consider in two categories.\r\n\r\nCategory 1: When a button has a form owner (is inside form or has a valid form attribute).\r\n\r\nCategory 2: When a button has no form owner.\r\n\r\n4 States:\r\n\r\nA) Button has a missing or invalid type attribute.\r\nB) Button as a type=\"submit\" attribute.\r\nC) Button has a type=\"reset\" attribute.\r\nD) Button has a type=\"button\" attribute.\r\n\r\nTable of behaviour when popovertarget is on each of these buttons.\r\n\r\n|         | Before  | After |\r\n| ------------- | ------------- | ------------- |\r\n| 1.A  | Form Submission  | Form Submission |\r\n| 1.B | Form Submission  | Form Submission |\r\n| 1.C | Form Reset And Popover Toggled* | Form Reset Only |\r\n| 1.D | Popover Toggled | Popover Toggled |\r\n| 2.A | Popover Toggled | Nothing |\r\n| 2.B | Popover Toggled | Nothing |\r\n| 2.C | Popover Toggled | Nothing |\r\n| 2.D | Popover Toggled | Popover Toggled |\r\n\r\n* Chromium actually only resets the form in this case current.\r\n\r\nThis would also introduce a change between how `<input popovertarget>` and `<button popovertarget>` behave.",
    "state": "CHANGES_REQUESTED",
